<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/"><channel><title>SQL Server on Andrea Azzola</title><link>https://andreaazzola.com/categories/sql-server/</link><description>Recent content in SQL Server on Andrea Azzola</description><generator>Hugo -- 0.148.2</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Fri, 06 Mar 2009 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://andreaazzola.com/categories/sql-server/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Clean Up Stored Procedures Cache with SQL Server</title><link>https://andreaazzola.com/post/clean-sp-cache-sql-server/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Mar 2009 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://andreaazzola.com/post/clean-sp-cache-sql-server/</guid><description>&lt;p>When deploying a stored procedure, cleaning up the database&amp;rsquo;s cache can help apply changes immediately. Have a look at the following code:&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;">&lt;code class="language-sql" data-lang="sql">&lt;span style="display:flex;">&lt;span>&lt;span style="color:#75715e">-- Cleans up db&amp;#39;s cache
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span style="display:flex;">&lt;span>&lt;span style="color:#75715e">&lt;/span>&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef">DECLARE&lt;/span> &lt;span style="color:#f92672">@&lt;/span>dbID INTEGER
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span style="display:flex;">&lt;span>&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef">SET&lt;/span> &lt;span style="color:#f92672">@&lt;/span>dbID &lt;span style="color:#f92672">=&lt;/span> (&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef">SELECT&lt;/span> dbid &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef">FROM&lt;/span> master.dbo.sysdatabases &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef">WHERE&lt;/span> name &lt;span style="color:#f92672">=&lt;/span> &lt;span style="color:#e6db74">&amp;#39;DBNameHere&amp;#39;&lt;/span>)
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span style="display:flex;">&lt;span>DBCC FLUSHPROCINDB (&lt;span style="color:#f92672">@&lt;/span>dbID)
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/div>&lt;p>The &lt;a href="http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/cc297250.aspx">DBCC FLUSHPROCINDB&lt;/a> command allows specifying a particular database id, and then clears all the plans from it.&lt;/p></description></item></channel></rss>